  description="A stateless gRPC authentication management service for the FICO Platform, processing 1M+ daily auth requests at a 99.98% uptime SLA."
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    The platform's identities ultimately live in a commercial identity provider,
    but "just call the IdP" does not scale as an integration strategy. Without a
    unifying layer, every platform service would integrate the IdP's REST API
    directly: inconsistent auth patterns, credentials managed ad hoc, no unified
    audit trail, and a hard coupling between every team's code and one vendor's
    API surface.
  </p>
  <p>
    The platform needed one contract-driven front door for authentication
    management: client credentials, SAML and OAuth federation, tenancy, and
    token introspection, behind stable, versioned interfaces. And because the
    consumers are enterprise financial institutions, the layer had to hold a
    99.98% uptime SLA and produce compliance-grade audit events, while sitting
    on top of an upstream dependency it does not control.
  </p>

  <h2>What I built</h2>
  <p>
    I own this service end to end: architectural design, the Spring Boot
    implementation, schema migrations, Helm-based deployment through GitHub
    Actions, and production operations including on-call. Alongside the core
    facade I designed and built a Kubernetes operator for declarative credential
    provisioning, an annotation-driven audit and metering system that publishes
    structured events through the platform's message bus, a configuration-driven
    feature-flag module, and the global exception mapping between HTTP and gRPC
    status codes.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    The service is a stateless proxy. It holds no database: the identity
    provider remains the single source of truth, and the service translates
    between protobuf-defined gRPC contracts on one side and the IdP's REST API
    on the other. Consumers get strong typing, generated clients, and a stable
    contract; the vendor dependency is quarantined behind one boundary that can
    evolve independently.
  </p>

