  <p>
    The credentials operator extends the same declarative model I used for
    <a href="/work/kubernetes-rbac-operator/">RBAC provisioning</a>: a team
    declares a credential as a custom resource, and the operator reconciles it
    into existence. The interesting design problem was least privilege. The
    operator must create secrets in target tenants, but a standing service
    account with cross-tenant power is exactly the credential you never want to
    exist. Instead, each provisioning action creates a short-lived credential
    scoped to the one target tenant, uses it, and deletes it in a
    <code>finally</code> block, with compensating-transaction rollback if secret
    creation fails partway.
  </p>

  <h2>Trade-offs</h2>

  <Tradeoff decision="A stateless proxy with no local database over caching identity state locally">
    <span slot="bought">
      No dual-write consistency problem, ever. The identity provider stays the
      single source of truth, and the service scales horizontally with nothing
      to replicate, back up, or reconcile.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      Availability is coupled to the upstream. I mitigated the failure mode
      rather than the dependency: custom gRPC health checks report NOT_SERVING
      when the IdP is unreachable, so Kubernetes routes traffic away and the
      service degrades explicitly instead of erroring opaquely.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <Tradeoff decision="A gRPC-only API over offering REST to consumers">
    <span slot="bought">
      Protobuf contracts as living documentation, generated type-safe clients
      in every consumer, and HTTP/2 multiplexing on a high-volume path.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      Harder ad-hoc debugging, since you cannot poke a binary API with curl,
      and every consumer buys into gRPC tooling. Acceptable on a platform where
      service-to-service traffic is gRPC by convention already.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <Tradeoff decision="Ephemeral per-operation credentials over a standing privileged service account">
    <span slot="bought">
      The operator never holds broad cross-tenant access. Every provisioning
      action is scoped to one tenant and time-limited, so the worst case of a
      leak is one narrow credential that is already scheduled for deletion.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      Each provisioning operation pays for credential creation and cleanup, and
      the rollback path has to be correct: a failure between credential
      creation and secret creation must trigger compensating cleanup or it
      leaks the credential it just made.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <Tradeoff decision="Aspect-oriented audit and metering annotations over explicit calls in business logic">
    <span slot="bought">
      Compliance-grade audit events with uniform structure, added to an
      operation by annotating it. Business logic stays readable, and audit
      coverage cannot drift out of style from method to method.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      Implicit behavior. The control flow that publishes an audit event is not
      visible at the call site, which makes tracing and debugging genuinely
      harder for engineers new to the codebase.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The authentication foundation for the platform: 1M+ daily auth requests
      at a 99.98% uptime SLA, consumed by 15+ internal services.
    </li>
    <li>
      3+ enterprise financial institutions onboarded through its tenancy and
      federation APIs.
    </li>
    <li>
      Credential lifecycle is declarative: CRD in, scoped secret out, with
      rollback on partial failure.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I'd do differently</h2>
  <p>
    I would build resilience against the upstream dependency in from the start,
    circuit breakers rather than health checks alone, because the health check
    protects the platform from the service but nothing yet smooths the service's
    own experience of a flapping upstream. I would also write integration tests
    against a faked IdP from day one; a proxy's correctness lives entirely at
    its boundary, which is exactly the part unit tests cannot see.
  </p>

